Here is a basic implementation of an autocomplete component in React without relying on any third-party packages:
```
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const Autocomplete = ({ suggestions, onSelect }) => {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');
  const [suggestionsList, setSuggestionsList] = useState([]);
  const [activeSuggestionIndex, setActiveSuggestionIndex] = useState(-1);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      if (inputValue.length > 0) {
        const filteredSuggestions = suggestions.filter((suggestion) => {
          return suggestion.toLowerCase().includes(inputValue.toLowerCase());
        });
        setSuggestionsList(filteredSuggestions);
      } else {
        setSuggestionsList([]);
      }
    }, 300);
    return () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
    };
  }, [inputValue, suggestions]);

  const handleInputChange = (evt) => {
    setInputValue(evt.target.value);
  };

  const handleSuggestionsList(key) {
    setActiveSuggestionIndex(key);
  };

  const handleClick = () => {
    if (activeSuggestionIndex !== -1) {
      onSelect(suggestionsList[activeSuggestionIndex]);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={inputValue}
        onChange={handleInputChange}
        placeholder="Type to autocomplete"
      />
      {suggestionsList.length > 0 && (
        <ul>
          {suggestionsList.map((suggestion, index) => (
            <li
              key={index}
              className={activeSuggestionIndex === index ? 'active' : ''}
              onMouseDown={() => handleSuggestionsList(index)}
            >
              {suggestion}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
      <button onClick={handleClick}>Select</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Autocomplete;
```
Here's an explanation of how the component works:

1. The component takes two props: `suggestions` (an array of suggestions to autocomplete) and `onSelect` (a function to call when a suggestion is selected).
2. The component uses four state variables: `inputValue` (the current input value), `suggestionsList` (the filtered list of suggestions), `activeSuggestionIndex` (the currently active suggestion), and `prevInputValue` (the previous input value, used to detect changes).
3. The component uses `useEffect` to set a timeout when the input value changes. This timeout is used to filter the suggestions list based on the input value.
4. The component renders an input field, a list of suggestions (if any), and a button to select a suggestion.
5. When the input value changes, the component updates the `suggestionsList` state with the filtered list of suggestions.
6. When a suggestion is clicked, the component sets the `activeSuggestionIndex` state to the index of the clicked suggestion.
7. When the select button is clicked, the component calls the `onSelect` prop function with the currently active suggestion.

